diagnosis_text:
The user-reported symptom — "20.x machines and 120.x machines cannot reach each other" — is not confirmed by any live evidence. A topology-wide Phase 1 reachability probe shows 0% packet loss in both directions between pc0 (20.1.1.10) and pc12 (120.1.1.10), the sole endpoints in each address family.

The network is a hybrid OSPF + EIGRP topology. The "two routing languages" are OSPF (covering the r1–r10 backbone) and EIGRP AS 100 (covering r12 and r13). Router r11 is the deliberate "translator": it runs both protocols and redistributes EIGRP routes into OSPF (`redistribute eigrp` in its OSPF config). The reverse path is handled by a kernel static default route on r12 (`0.0.0.0/0 via 112.1.1.1`) pointing back to r11, which carries the EIGRP-side traffic into the OSPF fabric. All EIGRP adjacencies (r11↔r12 on 112.0.0.0/8, r11↔r13 on 103.0.0.0/8) are in Full state. r1 correctly holds 120.0.0.0/8 learned via OSPF from r11. The OSPF snapshot flags for r8 (area 0.0.0.2 "orphaned") are false positives: r8 is the sole ABR for a private stub area, connected to the backbone via a valid virtual link to r4 (confirmed Full on both endpoints) and a direct Full adjacency to r5 in area 0.0.0.1. r13 not running ospfd is expected (EIGRP only). r12 having an ospfd process with zero OSPF config is a FRR default-daemon-start artefact, not a fault. Phase A (infra/L2), Phase B (routing), and Phase D (pressure) all came back clean. No mechanism exists to explain the reported failure, and no historical trace (no flap scripts, no syslog entries, no counter anomalies) supports a transient past event. The reported symptom is unconfirmed.
=== END ===